      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n----\n\n### **Test-Specific Global State (`optind`) in Production Code**\n\n**Issue:**\nIn `util/elan/cli.cc`, `ParseCommandLine` resets `optind \u003d 1;` with the comment `// Reset getopt internal state in case it\u0027s called multiple times (e.g. tests)`.\n\nThere are two main issues with this approach:\n\n1. **Design Anti-pattern:** Production code shouldn\u0027t carry global state resets solely to accommodate the quirks of a unit testing framework.\n2. **glibc Quirk:** On GNU libc (which ChromeOS uses), setting `optind \u003d 1` resets the index but does *not* completely clear `getopt_long`\u0027s internal scanning state. A full reset actually requires setting `optind \u003d 0`.\n\nAdditionally, there\u0027s a related issue in the test fixture: `getopt_long` strictly expects POSIX `argv` semantics, meaning the array must be null-terminated (`argv[argc] \u003d\u003d nullptr`). The current `MakeArgv` helper in the tests misses this.\n\n**Recommendation:**\nKeep the test logic entirely within the test files.\n\n**1. In `util/elan/cli.cc`:**\nRemove the following lines:\n\n```cpp\n  // Reset getopt internal state in case it\u0027s called multiple times (e.g. tests)\n  optind \u003d 1;\n\n```\n\n**2. In `util/elan/cli_test.cc`:**\nManage the `optind` reset via the `SetUp()` override, and add the missing `nullptr` termination to your `MakeArgv` helper:\n\n```cpp\nclass CliTest : public ::testing::Test {\n protected:\n  void SetUp() override {\n    // Fully reset getopt_long internal state for glibc between test runs.\n    optind \u003d 0; \n  }\n\n  // Helper to safely convert string literals to mutable char* arrays\n  std::vector\u003cchar*\u003e MakeArgv(std::vector\u003cstd::string\u003e\u0026 args) {\n    std::vector\u003cchar*\u003e argv;\n    argv.reserve(args.size() + 1); // +1 to accommodate the null terminator\n    for (auto\u0026 arg : args) {\n      argv.push_back(arg.data());\n    }\n    // POSIX requirement: argv array must be null-terminated\n    argv.push_back(nullptr); \n    return argv;\n  }\n};\n\n```",
